+"""Resource PLANNING and driver-side ADMISSION for one windowed water
+transaction (shared-storage S5B; plan 5.3 of
+`docs/shared-storage/adoption-plan.md`, design
+`docs/shared-storage/resource-admission-design.md`).
+
+What this module is
+-------------------
+* `windowed_transaction_bytes(...)`: a PURE counting function. From
+  validated shapes and options it derives, stage by stage, a conservative
+  upper bound on the NEW host and device bytes that are simultaneously
+  live while `maple.water.apply_windowed_water_process_demand` (path
+  `"public"`) or the private paired transaction
+  (`maple.water.windowed_transaction`, path `"paired"`) runs on the
+  accepted S1--S4 source. It allocates no model array, touches no device,
+  reads no file and loops over no domain cell; it builds only small Python
+  objects (the plan, its mappings and strings).
+* `admit_windowed_transaction(plan, ...)`: a DECISION against injected
+  read-only availability queries. It allocates no model array, performs no
+  persistence write and mutates no physical state or store. The default
+  host query is the existing reclaim-aware
+  `maple.aeolian.cohorts.capacity.default_available_memory_bytes`
+  (a read-only operating-system read of `/proc/meminfo` and, where
+  present, cgroup-v2 files); there is NO default device query -- a device
+  plan without an injected effective-free query is refused.
+
+What it is NOT
+--------------
+* Not a reservation. Admitting a step cannot stop another process, thread
+  or the allocator from taking memory between the decision and the
+  allocation. A driver that wants a reservation must serialize its own
+  steps and keep its own margin.
+* Not automatic. Nothing in the dense or windowed steps calls this module;
+  a transaction that is run without a preceding driver call to the
+  admission is exactly as unprotected as before S5B. The driver that owns
+  the windowed step loop (a SYRUP-side driver, a MAPLE test driver) calls
+  `windowed_transaction_bytes` and `admit_windowed_transaction` BEFORE the
+  chosen transaction, with the same options it will pass to the step.
+* Not a whole-process bound. The figure covers NATIVE ARRAY allocations
+  of the transaction only. Explicitly OUTSIDE the bound and left to the
+  driver's `reserve_*_bytes` (which are the driver's own figures, never a
+  guarantee): publication (`publish_windowed_result` / `apply_journal`
+  chunk and manifest buffers), the windowed commit, external transport,
+  the CuPy memory pool's retained blocks, Numba/CUDA JIT and library
+  workspaces, Python object overhead, deep-store memmap page cache beyond
+  `mapped_bytes_max` (reported separately; file-backed, reclaimable).
+
+How the bound is derived (so a reviewer can audit it)
+-----------------------------------------------------
+Every term is a closed form in the validated counts, named after the
+source symbol that allocates it, under a CONSERVATIVE lifetime rule: an
+array a function binds to a name is live from its statement to the
+function's return unless the source explicitly `del`s it; an unnamed
+temporary is live for its statement; a result object keeps its arrays
+live for the caller; when a name is REBOUND to a new array the old array
+is counted as live while the new one is built (right-hand-side overlap).
+Where a lifetime is uncertain the term is summed, not maximised, and the
+overestimate is stated in the provenance. Kernel-scratch terms (the dense
+batched kernels, the proportional vector, the validators, the E1
+encode/advance) come ONLY from the frozen module constant
+`KERNEL_SCRATCH_BOUNDS`, each entry carrying the statements it counts;
+the planner has no parameter through which a caller could supply, lower
+or zero one. A required entry that is absent is listed in the plan's
+`unbounded_terms` and makes the plan inadmissible by name. Measurements
+(`tests/shared_storage/test_windowed_admission_coverage.py`) VERIFY that
+observed simultaneous new live bytes fit under the derived bound; they
+never define or tighten it.
+
+Windowed memory scaling is preserved: a miss batch is at most
+`max_gather_columns` full columns (plus the right-hand-side overlap of
+ONE previous batch), never all cells' full columns and never the
+cumulative traffic of every batch; the journal is bounded by the caller's
+`max_journal_records` AND by what the step can actually append.
+
+Paired path
+-----------
+The admitted figure of a `"paired"` plan ALWAYS covers the allocating
+fallback: the phase-P retained private set (column, cache totals,
+encoded base, pending journal copy, layer, ledger, availability, the
+callback's view) and the callback's returned arrays are live while the
+deposit phase runs the same stage sequence as the public step with a
+COPYING begin and two ALLOCATING accumulations. Whether the owner adopts
+or donates is decided INSIDE the step and reported only afterwards, so
+that saving is reported as `advisory_owned_*_bytes_peak` for planning and
+diagnostics only; admission never reads it. No no-fallback mode and no
+runtime ownership API exist.
